mk("AUTHORS","Author","Virginia Woolf","Pioneered stream-of-consciousness technique in novels like 'Mrs Dalloway' (1925), which takes place in a single day. She and her husband Leonard founded Hogarth Press — they hand-printed their first book on their dining table. Her essay 'A Room of One's Own' (1929) argued that a woman needs money and a room of her own to write fiction.","","Mrs Dalloway, first section",28,"Read the opening pages. Clarissa Dalloway walks through London buying flowers for her party. Woolf makes a single June day contain an entire life — memories of a summer at Bourton, a kiss, a choice made 30 years ago. Notice how the narration drifts between characters like a bee visiting flowers: Clarissa, then Septimus, then back.",["British","Fiction","Modernist","20th Century"],"virginia-woolf"),

mk("AUTHORS","Author","Franz Kafka","Prague-born writer who worked at an insurance company by day and wrote at night. He made his friend Max Brod promise to burn all his manuscripts after his death — Brod published them instead. His stories about bureaucracies crushing individuals gave us the word 'Kafkaesque.' He died of tuberculosis at 40, mostly unpublished.","","The Metamorphosis, the whole novella",25,"Read the whole novella — about 60 pages. Gregor Samsa wakes up as a giant insect. His first thought: how will I get to work? Kafka never explains the transformation; the horror is the family's reaction, not the bug. Notice the deadpan tone — Kafka tells the impossible like a police report. Gregor's humanity fades as his family's indifference grows.",["Czech","Fiction","Modernist","20th Century"],"franz-kafka"),

