b3 = [
mk("BOOKS","Book","One Hundred Years of Solitude (1967)","Garcia Marquez's novel follows the Buendia family over seven generations in the town of Macondo. The opening line collapses past, present, and future into one sentence. Over 50 million copies sold. The novel was written over 18 months while Garcia Marquez's wife pawned their car and furniture to buy food and paper.","Gabriel Garcia Marquez","One Hundred Years of Solitude, chapters 1-5",50,"Read the first five chapters. Jose Arcadio Buendia founds Macondo, and the gypsy Melquiades arrives with magnets, telescopes, and ice. Garcia Marquez reports miracles as casually as the weather — Remedios ascends to heaven while doing laundry, a rain of yellow flowers, insomnia that erases memories. Notice the circular names: generations repeat, and time is a spiral, not a line.",["Colombian","Magical Realism","Literary","20th Century"],"one-hundred-years-of-solitude"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Pride and Prejudice (1813)","Jane Austen's novel of manners published anonymously — the title page says only 'By the Author of Sense and Sensibility.' Elizabeth Bennet and Mr. Darcy's love story is built on mutual misjudgment. The novel sold for £110 in its first edition and has never been out of print. Austen called it 'my own darling child.'","Jane Austen","Pride and Prejudice, volume 1 (chapters 1-23)",55,"Read Volume I. Elizabeth Bennet is one of five daughters, and the estate is entailed away to Mr. Collins. Austen's narrator is ironic: 'It is a truth universally acknowledged' is a joke. Notice the free indirect discourse — the narrator slips into Elizabeth's mind without signaling, so you share her prejudice against Darcy before you know she's wrong. The proposal scene in the rain is the novel's fault line.",["British","Romance","Classic","19th Century"],"pride-and-prejudice"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","To Kill a Mockingbird (1960)","Harper Lee's only published novel for 55 years (until 'Go Set a Watchman' in 2015). It won the Pulitzer and has sold 40+ million copies. Lee based Scout's friend Dill on her childhood neighbor Truman Capote. She gave her manuscript to an editor who said it might sell 2,500 copies. She never wrote another book and refused almost all interviews.","Harper Lee","To Kill a Mockingbird, Part One",40,"Read Part One. Scout Finch narrates from childhood, looking back at one summer in Maycomb, Alabama. Boo Radley is a ghost in the house next door; the children act out his story. Notice the point of view: Scout is 6, but her narration is filtered through adult understanding. The trial of Tom Robinson is the center, but the novel earns it through the slow accumulation of small-town rhythms.",["American","Fiction","Classic","20th Century"],"to-kill-a-mockingbird"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","1984 (1949)","Orwell's final novel, written while he was dying of tuberculosis on a remote Scottish island. It was originally called 'The Last Man in Europe.' Every surveillance fear in the novel — telescreens, thought police, newspeak — was Orwell's extrapolation of wartime propaganda and Stalinist purges. The adjective 'Orwellian' entered English within a decade.","George Orwell","1984, Part One",35,"Read Part One. Winston Smith lives in Oceania where Big Brother watches. He commits his first thoughtcrime: buying a blank diary. Orwell builds the horror through details — Victory Gin that tastes like nitric acid, a torn poster that hides the alcove, the Ministry of Truth where Winston rewrites history. Notice the Appendix on Newspeak at the end of the book: the clock is still ticking.",["British","Dystopian","Political","20th Century"],"nineteen-eighty-four"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Master and Margarita (1967)","Mikhail Bulgakov's satirical novel written during Stalin's terror, hidden in a drawer, and published 26 years after his death. The devil visits 1930s Moscow with a talking cat. The novel alternates between Soviet mockery and the story of Pontius Pilate. When it was finally published in a censored magazine, readers photocopied the missing sections and glued them in.","Mikhail Bulgakov","The Master and Margarita, Book One",50,"Read Book One. Woland (the devil) arrives in Moscow and predicts a man's beheading by tram — which happens minutes later. His entourage includes a giant talking cat called Behemoth who drinks vodka and rides the trolley. Bulgakov wrote this knowing it would never be published in his lifetime. The satire of Soviet bureaucracy is so sharp it still draws blood. Notice Pilate's story — it's the novel within the novel.",["Russian","Satire","Fantasy","20th Century"],"the-master-and-margarita"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Catcher in the Rye (1951)","J.D. Salinger's novel about 72 hours in the life of Holden Caulfield after being expelled from prep school. It's been banned more than almost any other American novel — for language, for sexuality, for 'undermining authority.' Salinger became a recluse in New Hampshire and never published another book. Holden's red hunting hat has become an icon of adolescent alienation.","J.D. Salinger","The Catcher in the Rye, chapters 1-10",35,"Read the first ten chapters. Holden narrates from a sanatorium in California. He's been expelled from Pencey Prep and decided to leave early. Every sentence is in Holden's voice — sarcastic, vulnerable, obsessed with 'phonies.' Notice the red hunting hat: he wears it backwards, it makes him feel safe, and he keeps offering it to others. The ducks in Central Park: where do they go in winter? Holden keeps asking — no one answers.",["American","Fiction","Coming-of-Age","20th Century"],"the-catcher-in-the-rye"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Anna Karenina (1878)","Tolstoy's novel about a married aristocrat who leaves her husband for a cavalry officer. The novel opens with the most famous first sentence in literature: 'Happy families are all alike; every unhappy family is unhappy in its own way.' Tolstoy originally planned a novella about an adulterous wife but kept adding — the novel now spans 800+ pages and is as much about Levin farming as Anna's tragedy.","Leo Tolstoy","Anna Karenina, Part One",48,"Read Part One. Anna arrives at the Moscow train station and meets Vronsky. A man falls under a train — bad omen. Meanwhile Levin proposes to Kitty and is rejected. Tolstoy writes consciousness like no one else: Anna's inner life is a series of sudden shifts — a feeling, then the opposite feeling. Notice how the train becomes the novel's symbol: arrival, escape, and in the end, what Anna throws herself under.",["Russian","Fiction","Classic","19th Century"],"anna-karenina"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Brief Wondrous Life of Oscar Wao (2007)","Junot Diaz's novel about an overweight Dominican nerd growing up in New Jersey who dreams of becoming the Dominican J.R.R. Tolkien. The novel won the Pulitzer. The footnotes — lengthy, profane, scholarly — explain Dominican history from Trujillo to the diaspora. Diaz mixes Spanglish, sci-fi references, and deep political history in every paragraph.","Junot Diaz","The Brief Wondrous Life of Oscar Wao, Part I",38,"Read Part I. The narrator Yunior tells Oscar's story — a ghetto nerd who falls in love too hard and too fast. The footnotes are as long as the page text and cover the Trujillo regime, the fuku (curse), and Dominican nerd culture. Diaz writes like he's telling you a story at 2 AM — profane, tender, full of digressions that circle back. Oscar's tragedy is that he's a romantic in a world that punishes sincerity.",["Dominican-American","Fiction","Contemporary","21st Century"],"the-brief-wondrous-life-of-oscar-wao"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Wind-Up Bird Chronicle (1994)","Haruki Murakami's novel about a man searching for his missing cat and then his missing wife — and falling into a well, literally and metaphorically. Set partly during the Japanese occupation of Manchuria, the novel connects personal disappearance to historical atrocity. The wind-up bird is invisible but its cry — like a spring winding — drives the plot.","Haruki Murakami","The Wind-Up Bird Chronicle, Book One: The Thieving Magpie",45,"Read Book One. Toru Okada quits his job, makes spaghetti, and receives a strange phone call from a woman who says she knows him. Then his cat disappears. Then his wife. Murakami writes the surreal with the same flat affect as the mundane — a woman who talks through a medium, a dry well, a mark on a man's cheek. Notice how WWII atrocities surface in Toru's personal story without ever feeling forced.",["Japanese","Fiction","Magical Realism","20th Century"],"the-wind-up-bird-chronicle"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Frankenstein (1818)","Mary Shelley wrote this novel when she was 18 — on a dare. During a rainy summer at Lake Geneva, Lord Byron challenged everyone to write a ghost story. Shelley had a waking nightmare of a 'pale student of unhallowed arts' and transformed it into the first science fiction novel. Published anonymously, many assumed her husband Percy had written it.","Mary Shelley","Frankenstein, Volume I (chapters 1-8)",40,"Read Volume I. Victor Frankenstein creates life and immediately abandons it in horror. The creature learns language by spying on a family through a hole in the wall — reading Paradise Lost and Plutarch's Lives. Notice the novel's structure: a frame narrative (letters from an Arctic explorer) within a frame (Victor's confession) within the creature's own story. Who is the monster? Mary Shelley lets you decide.",["British","Gothic","Science Fiction","19th Century"],"frankenstein"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Color Purple (1982)","Alice Walker's novel told through letters — first Celie's letters to God, then letters between Celie and her sister Nettie. It won the Pulitzer and the National Book Award. The novel spans 40 years of Celie's life in rural Georgia, from abuse to liberation. Walker's use of Black Southern vernacular was revolutionary — Celie's voice grows in complexity as she ages.","Alice Walker","The Color Purple, first 40 letters",42,"Read the first 40 letters. Celie is 14 and pregnant by her father — she writes to God because she has no one else. The spelling is phonetic: 'I am fourteen years old. I have always been a good girl.' As Celie ages, her voice matures — the letters become longer, more analytical, more powerful. Notice the silence that surrounds female desire: Shug Avery teaches Celie that pleasure is possible. The novel earns its joy.",["American","Fiction","Epistolary","20th Century"],"the-color-purple"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Sapiens (2014)","Yuval Noah Harari's nonfiction history of humankind — from the Cognitive Revolution (70,000 years ago) to the present. Originally published in Hebrew, it became an international bestseller with 23+ million copies sold. Harari argues that what made Homo sapiens dominant was our ability to believe in fictions: money, nations, laws, gods.","Yuval Noah Harari","Sapiens, Part One: The Cognitive Revolution",45,"Read Part One. Harari argues that 70,000 years ago, Sapiens developed language capable of describing things that don't exist — which let us cooperate in huge numbers. Notice his method: each chapter begins with a big claim, supports it with evidence, then undercuts it with a question. The book makes you feel both proud and horrified to be human — the Agricultural Revolution, Harari argues, was 'history's biggest fraud.'",["Israeli","Nonfiction","History","21st Century"],"sapiens"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","A Brief History of Time (1988)","Stephen Hawking's book about cosmology — from the Big Bang to black holes — written for general readers. It stayed on the Sunday Times bestseller list for 237 weeks. Hawking wrote it while almost completely paralyzed by ALS, dictating through a speech synthesizer. The book has sold over 25 million copies though many admit they never finished it.","Stephen Hawking","A Brief History of Time, chapters 1-3",40,"Read the first three chapters. Hawking starts with Aristotle and Ptolemy — our picture of the universe as a series of nested spheres. Then Galileo, Newton, Einstein, and the expanding universe. Notice how Hawking builds each concept on the last: you don't need math to follow the argument, just curiosity. The question at the center: why does the universe bother to exist?",["British","Nonfiction","Science","20th Century"],"a-brief-history-of-time"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Demon Copperhead (2022)","Barbara Kingsolver's novel reimagining David Copperfield in modern Appalachia — a boy born to a teenage mother in a single-wide trailer in Lee County, Virginia. Won the Pulitzer and the Women's Prize. The novel is a direct response to the opioid crisis and the failure of institutions meant to protect children. Demon's voice is the novel's engine: wry, unbroken, irresistible.","Barbara Kingsolver","Demon Copperhead, first 10 chapters",50,"Read the first ten chapters. Demon is born in a trailer to a teenage mother who ODs early. He enters foster care — the system is the villain. Kingsolver follows Dickens's chapter structure exactly. Notice Demon's voice: he's telling his story as an adult looking back, with humor that never softens the horror. The foster homes, the child labor, the first pill — the novel is an indictment that reads like a page-turner.",["American","Fiction","Contemporary","21st Century"],"demon-copperhead"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Secret History (1992)","Donna Tartt's debut novel about a group of elite Classics students at a Vermont college who murder one of their own. The novel tells you who dies and who kills him on the first page — the suspense is why. Tartt wrote it over eight years at Bennington College. The novel launched the 'dark academia' aesthetic and has sold millions.","Donna Tartt","The Secret History, Book I",45,"Read Book I. Richard Papen arrives at Hampden College from a working-class California background and falls into a clique of beautiful, wealthy, morally untethered Classics students. Their professor Julian teaches them to live like the ancients. The first murder is presented as ritual, then necessity. Notice Tartt's atmosphere: the Vermont winter, the Greek declensions, the opium tea — beauty and decay are inseparable.",["American","Fiction","Mystery","20th Century"],"the-secret-history"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Parable of the Sower (1993)","Octavia Butler's dystopian novel set in the 2020s — a world of climate collapse, corporate towns, and a populist president who promises to 'Make America Great Again.' The protagonist Lauren Olamina founds a religion called Earthseed whose first verse is 'God is Change.' Butler predicted our present with eerie accuracy from 1993. She died in 2006 at 58.","Octavia Butler","Parable of the Sower, first 5 chapters",35,"Read the first five chapters. Lauren is 15 when her walled community is destroyed. Her journal entries open each chapter with an Earthseed verse. She has 'hyperempathy' — she feels others' pain physically — which is a fatal weakness in a world of constant violence. The novel is a survival story, but the real engine is Lauren's idea: that change is the only constant, and the destination is the stars.",["American","Science Fiction","Dystopian","20th Century"],"parable-of-the-sower"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Sympathizer (2015)","Viet Thanh Nguyen's debut novel won the Pulitzer. It's a confession written by a half-French, half-Vietnamese communist spy who infiltrates the South Vietnamese army and flees to Los Angeles after the fall of Saigon. The novel is one long sentence of guilt, irony, and savage critique of both American intervention and Vietnamese communism.","Viet Thanh Nguyen","The Sympathizer, chapters 1-5",45,"Read the first five chapters. The narrator is a captain in the South Vietnamese army and a communist mole. He escapes Saigon on one of the last helicopters and lands in Los Angeles. Nguyen writes the entire novel in a confessional voice — the Captain is writing a forced confession in a reeducation camp. Notice the double vision: every event is seen from two ideological perspectives simultaneously. 'I am a spy, a sleeper, a spook, a man of two faces.'",["Vietnamese-American","Fiction","Historical","21st Century"],"the-sympathizer"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Klara and the Sun (2021)","Kazuo Ishiguro's first novel after winning the Nobel Prize. It's narrated by Klara, an Artificial Friend — a solar-powered robot companion for children — who observes the world with luminous naivety. The novel is about love, sacrifice, and what happens when technology offers to replace what we've lost. Ishiguro said he wrote the book because he wanted to explore what it means to love without jealousy.","Kazuo Ishiguro","Klara and the Sun, Part One",38,"Read Part One. Klara stands in a store waiting to be chosen. She's solar-powered and worships the Sun as a god — she believes it gives her 'special nourishment.' Ishiguro writes from Klara's limited perspective: she processes the world in grids and boxes, and human emotions are puzzles she must solve. Notice how the prose never signals tragedy — like all Ishiguro narrators, Klara's innocence is the novel's devastating tool.",["British","Science Fiction","Literary","21st Century"],"klara-and-the-sun"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Dune (1965)","Frank Herbert's science fiction novel set on the desert planet Arrakis — the only source of the spice melange, the most valuable substance in the universe. It was rejected by 20 publishers before being accepted by a publisher of car repair manuals. It's the best-selling science fiction novel of all time and inspired Star Wars, Game of Thrones, and ecological movements.","Frank Herbert","Dune, Book One: Dune",55,"Read Book One. Paul Atreides, ducal heir, arrives on Arrakis with his family — a trap set by their enemies. Herbert built a world as detailed as Tolkien's: the Fremen (desert dwellers), the sandworms, the stillsuit that recycles body moisture. Notice the ecological theme — every detail of Fremen culture is an adaptation to water scarcity. Paul's journey from boy to messiah is not heroic — it's a warning about charismatic leaders.",["American","Science Fiction","Epic","20th Century"],"dune"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Goldfinch (2013)","Donna Tartt's novel about a boy who survives a terrorist bombing at the Metropolitan Museum of Art and steals a 17th-century painting — Carel Fabritius's 'The Goldfinch.' The novel won the Pulitzer. It's 771 pages of grief, art theft, antique furniture restoration, and a friendship with a Ukrainian boy named Boris who may be the most alive character in contemporary fiction.","Donna Tartt","The Goldfinch, Part I",50,"Read Part I. Theo Decker, 13, loses his mother in a museum bombing. Dazed, he takes the painting — a small goldfinch chained to its perch. Tartt writes childhood trauma with Proustian density: every object in Theo's life becomes saturated with meaning. Boris, his school friend in Las Vegas, introduces Theo to vodka, drugs, and a philosophy of survival: 'We are two cockroaches, my friend.' The painting becomes Theo's religion.",["American","Fiction","Literary","21st Century"],"the-goldfinch"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Slaughterhouse-Five (1969)","Kurt Vonnegut's anti-war novel based on his experience as a POW during the firebombing of Dresden — he was one of the few American soldiers to witness it, sheltered in an underground meat locker. The novel's structure — time-hopping, alien abduction — was Vonnegut's way of writing about trauma that couldn't be narrated linearly. He wrote it 23 years after the bombing.","Kurt Vonnegut","Slaughterhouse-Five, chapters 1-5",30,"Read the first five chapters. Billy Pilgrim has 'come unstuck in time.' The novel opens with Vonnegut as a character trying to write the book. Billy's time-traveling is not science fiction — it's PTSD. Each jump is triggered by something in the present: a barbershop quartet reminds him of the German soldiers grinning at the destruction of Dresden. 'So it goes' — the novel's refrain — appears 106 times. Every death is equal.",["American","Satire","Anti-War","20th Century"],"slaughterhouse-five"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Americanah (2013)","Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie's novel about Ifemelu and Obinze, Nigerian teenagers in love who emigrate separately — she to America, he to London. Ifemelu starts a blog about race in America called 'Raceteenth.' The novel is about love, hair, race as a construct, and the decision to go home. Adichie said she wrote it because she was tired of reading American books where race was invisible.","Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie","Americanah, Part One",42,"Read Part One. Ifemelu, now a successful blogger in America, decides to return to Nigeria and braids her hair for the journey — the braiding salon chapters are a frame around the story. Adichie captures the shock of encountering American racial categories as an immigrant: 'I did not think of myself as black and I only became black when I came to America.' Notice the blog posts embedded in the novel — they're sharp, funny, and true.",["Nigerian","Fiction","Contemporary","21st Century"],"americanah"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Road (2006)","Cormac McCarthy's post-apocalyptic novel about a father and son walking through a dead America. The world has burned — nothing grows, no birds sing, and the only survivors are 'the good guys' and the cannibals. McCarthy wrote it for his own young son. Won the Pulitzer. The novel strips language to its bones: no chapter numbers, no quotation marks, barely any proper nouns.","Cormac McCarthy","The Road, the first half",35,"Read the first half. 'When he woke in the woods in the dark and the cold of the night he'd reach out to touch the child sleeping beside him.' The man and the boy push a shopping cart of scavenged goods. Their dialogue is minimalist, liturgical: 'We're carrying the fire.' Notice what McCarthy omits — character names, explanations of the catastrophe, interior monologue. The love is in what they do, not what they say.",["American","Fiction","Post-Apocalyptic","21st Century"],"the-road"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Underground Railroad (2016)","Colson Whitehead's novel that turns the metaphorical Underground Railroad into a literal train system running beneath the South — engineers, boxcars, tunnels. Won the Pulitzer and the National Book Award. Whitehead said the premise came from a childish misunderstanding: as a kid, he imagined the Railroad was real, and he wanted to honor that literal belief.","Colson Whitehead","The Underground Railroad, chapters 1-4",40,"Read the first four chapters. Cora, a slave on a Georgia cotton plantation, escapes on the Underground Railroad — an actual train beneath the soil. Each state she visits is a different iteration of American racism: South Carolina performs a genteel eugenics, North Carolina bans Black people entirely. Whitehead's prose is measured, terrifying, and unflinching. The railroad is a magnificent lie made real — and each stop is a new horror.",["American","Historical Fiction","Speculative","21st Century"],"the-underground-railroad"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Erasure (2001)","Percival Everett's novel about a Black novelist who, enraged by the success of stereotypical 'ghetto fiction,' writes a parody of it under a pseudonym — which becomes a smash hit that the literary establishment takes seriously. The novel was adapted into the Oscar-winning film 'American Fiction' (2023).","Percival Everett","Erasure, first half",40,"Read the first half. Thelonious 'Monk' Ellison writes dense, literary novels that don't sell. Under the name Stagg R. Leigh, he writes 'My Pafology' (later retitled 'Fuck') — every stereotype in one book. The novel-within-a-novel is included in full — and it's hilarious and painful in equal measure. Notice how the publishing industry's embrace of the fake book implicates the reader too.",["American","Fiction","Satire","21st Century"],"erasure"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Piranesi (2020)","Susanna Clarke's novel about a man living in an infinite House with marble halls, tides, and statues. He calls himself Piranesi, though that's not his name. He keeps meticulous journals and believes he's one of only two living people in the world. Clarke wrote it while housebound with chronic fatigue syndrome. It won the Women's Prize.","Susanna Clarke","Piranesi, Part One",30,"Read Part One. 'I am the Beloved Child of the House.' Piranesi explores the endless halls, catalogues the statues, fishes, and writes in his journals. His narration is gentle, precise, and happy — which is why the dawning horror is so effective. The House is both prison and paradise. Notice the capitalized nouns: the House, the Tides, the Other — Piranesi's worldview is a cosmology of one. The mystery of who he really is unfolds through journal entries.",["British","Fantasy","Literary","21st Century"],"piranesi"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","My Brilliant Friend (2011)","Elena Ferrante's first Neapolitan Novel — about Lila and Elena, two girls growing up in a poor Naples neighborhood in the 1950s. Lila is brilliant and dangerous; Elena is the diligent one who gets out. Their friendship is the central love story. The identity of the author — 'Elena Ferrante' is a pseudonym — remains unknown, though a journalist claimed to unmask her in 2016.","Elena Ferrante","My Brilliant Friend, Childhood + Adolescence",50,"Read the Childhood and Adolescence sections. Lila throws Elena's doll into a cellar. Elena does the same to Lila's. They descend into the darkness to retrieve them and Lila doesn't come back — the first of many disappearances. Ferrante writes girlhood as a war fought with grades, shoes, and the attention of boys. Notice the neighborhood (the rione) — it's a character, violent and intimate. Escape is possible, but the rione never lets you go.",["Italian","Fiction","Literary","21st Century"],"my-brilliant-friend"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Station Eleven (2014)","Emily St. John Mandel's novel about a pandemic that collapses civilization — written years before COVID. A traveling Shakespeare troupe performs for survivor settlements 20 years after a Georgian flu killed 99% of humanity. The novel jumps between the pre-pandemic world and the post, connected by a graphic novel called 'Station Eleven' drawn by the actor Arthur Leander's first wife.","Emily St. John Mandel","Station Eleven, Parts 1-3",40,"Read Parts 1-3. Arthur Leander dies of a heart attack onstage during King Lear. That same night, the flu arrives. Twenty years later, Kirsten — a child actress who was on stage with Arthur that night — performs Shakespeare in the post-pandemic wilderness. Mandel makes the apocalypse beautiful: 'Survival is insufficient.' Notice the connections — every character is linked through Arthur, and survival depends on art.",["Canadian","Science Fiction","Literary","21st Century"],"station-eleven"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Odyssey (c. 8th century BCE)","The ancient Greek epic attributed to Homer — Odysseus's 10-year journey home from the Trojan War. Penelope fends off 108 suitors; Odysseus blinds the Cyclops, resists the Sirens, and descends into the underworld. Every road-trip story, every 'getting home' narrative, descends from this poem. It was composed to be sung, not read.","Homer","The Odyssey, Books 1-6",50,"Read Books 1-6. Odysseus is on Calypso's island, captive and weeping. Meanwhile in Ithaca, Penelope unweaves her father-in-law's shroud each night to delay choosing a new husband. Telemachus, the son who never knew his father, sets out to find news. Notice how Homer starts in the middle — the story is told through flashbacks and songs. The gods are childish, cruel, and hilarious. Athena's disguise as Mentor gave us the word.",["Greek","Epic Poetry","Classical","Ancient"],"the-odyssey"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings (1969)","Maya Angelou's first memoir — covering her childhood from age 3 to 17 in Stamps, Arkansas. She was raped at 7 by her mother's boyfriend; after she testified and he was killed, she stopped speaking for five years, believing her voice had killed him. The memoir launched her career and was the first nonfiction bestseller by a Black woman.","Maya Angelou","I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings, chapters 1-15",45,"Read the first 15 chapters. Marguerite (later Maya) and her brother Bailey are sent alone on a train at ages 3 and 4 from California to Arkansas with tags pinned to their coats. Angelou's voice is the memoir's gift — she writes from childhood without simplifying childhood. The five years of silence after the assault: she read everything, memorized Shakespeare, and discovered that language could be a refuge. The caged bird sings not despite the cage but because of it.",["American","Memoir","Autobiography","20th Century"],"i-know-why-the-caged-bird-sings"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy (1979)","Douglas Adams's comic science fiction novel that started as a BBC radio series. Arthur Dent is saved from Earth's destruction by his friend Ford Prefect — an alien researcher for the titular guidebook. The answer to life, the universe, and everything is 42. Adams wrote the first draft drunk in a field in Innsbruck. The novel has sold 14+ million copies.","Douglas Adams","The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy, chapters 1-10",30,"Read the first ten chapters. Arthur Dent's house is about to be demolished to make way for a bypass — the same morning the Earth is destroyed to make way for an interstellar bypass. Adams's humor is equal parts philosophy and absurdity. Notice the Guide entries — they're the funniest parts of the book, satirizing the tone of tourist guidebooks. The Vogon poetry scene is the worst torture in the galaxy. Don't panic.",["British","Science Fiction","Comedy","20th Century"],"the-hitchhikers-guide-to-the-galaxy"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Half of a Yellow Sun (2006)","Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie's novel about the Biafran War (1967-70), told through three characters: Ugwu, a houseboy; Olanna, a wealthy woman; and Richard, a British writer. Adichie grew up in the house where the Biafran leader once lived; her grandfathers fought on opposite sides. The novel won the Women's Prize and was voted the best book to have won the award.","Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie","Half of a Yellow Sun, Part One: The Early Sixties",48,"Read Part One. The novel opens with Ugwu, a village boy, becoming a houseboy for Odenigbo, a radical professor. Olanna moves from Lagos to live with Odenigbo. The first half is domestic — dinner parties, love affairs, academic debates — and Adichie makes you love these characters before the war comes. Notice the shattering mid-novel reveal: the book has been withholding the worst thing that happens.",["Nigerian","Historical Fiction","Literary","21st Century"],"half-of-a-yellow-sun"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Virgin Suicides (1993)","Jeffrey Eugenides's debut novel about the five Lisbon sisters who kill themselves over the course of a year in 1970s suburban Michigan. The novel is narrated by the neighborhood boys, now middle-aged, still obsessed and clueless. Eugenides wrote it in a single summer while working as a secretary.","Jeffrey Eugenides","The Virgin Suicides, the whole novel",35,"Read the whole novel — it's short, under 250 pages. The boys narrate as a collective 'we' — they never become individuals. They watch the Lisbon girls from across the street, collect their belongings, and 20 years later still can't understand. Notice the epistolary elements: doctor's reports, diary entries, photographs. The girls remain opaque — the novel is about the failure to understand, and the beauty in that failure. 'They made us participate in their own madness.'",["American","Fiction","Literary","20th Century"],"the-virgin-suicides"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Crying in H Mart (2021)","Michelle Zauner's memoir about losing her Korean mother to cancer and finding her identity in Korean food. Zauner is better known as the musician Japanese Breakfast. The book became a #1 New York Times bestseller. H Mart is an Asian supermarket chain — the title comes from the experience of crying in the food court because every item reminds you of your dead mother.","Michelle Zauner","Crying in H Mart, the whole memoir",45,"Read the whole memoir. Zauner's mother is diagnosed with cancer when Michelle is 25. The memoir moves between the illness, Zauner's childhood in Eugene, Oregon, her rebellious teenage years, and the ritual of cooking Korean food to keep her mother present. Notice the food descriptions — they're precise, physical, and spiritual. Making kimchi becomes a form of grief. The book is a reminder that love lives in the body and the kitchen.",["Korean-American","Memoir","Nonfiction","21st Century"],"crying-in-h-mart"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Power (2016)","Nina Alderman's novel about a world where women suddenly develop the ability to emit electric shocks from their hands — upending every power structure. The novel is framed as a manuscript written 5,000 years in the future by a male historian in a matriarchal world. Won the Women's Prize. It reads like a thriller and thinks like a philosophical treatise.","Naomi Alderman","The Power, Book One",38,"Read Book One. Teenage girls around the world discover the power — a skein of electricity they can discharge at will. The novel follows four characters: a British foster kid, an American politician's daughter, a Nigerian journalist, and a Moldovan trafficker's son. Alderman makes the premise chillingly plausible — within weeks, the world reorganizes. Notice the frame: letters between 'Neil' and 'Naomi' in a future where men are the weaker sex, and their historical revisionism is the mirror of ours.",["British","Science Fiction","Feminist","21st Century"],"the-power"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Homegoing (2016)","Yaa Gyasi's debut novel spanning 300 years — from the Gold Coast (Ghana) to modern America. Each chapter is a new generation descended from two half-sisters: one married to a British slave trader, one sold into slavery. Gyasi wrote it at 25 after a research trip to Ghana. The novel does in 300 pages what most family sagas do in 800.","Yaa Gyasi","Homegoing, the whole novel",50,"Read the whole novel — each chapter is a generation. Effia marries a British governor of Cape Coast Castle; her half-sister Esi is held in the dungeon below the same castle, then shipped to America. Each chapter is a complete short story about one descendant. The structure is a chain: you lose each character after one chapter, but their children carry the inheritance. Notice the recurring fire and water imagery — it runs through the bloodline like a scar.",["Ghanaian-American","Historical Fiction","Literary","21st Century"],"homegoing"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Vanishing Half (2020)","Brit Bennett's novel about identical twin sisters from a small Black town in Louisiana — one lives as a Black woman, the other passes for white. Their daughters' lives intersect years later. The novel explores race as performance, identity as choice, and what we owe to where we came from. It was an instant #1 bestseller and HBO adapted it.","Brit Bennett","The Vanishing Half, Parts I-II",40,"Read Parts I and II. The Vignes twins run away from Mallard, Louisiana — a town so light-skinned it was founded on colorism. Desiree returns years later with a 'blue-black' daughter. Stella has disappeared into whiteness — her new life in Brentwood, California is built on a lie she can never confess. Bennett writes passing not as deception but as grief: Stella loses her twin, her mother, her town, and eventually herself.",["American","Fiction","Literary","21st Century"],"the-vanishing-half"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","White Teeth (2000)","Zadie Smith's debut novel written during her final year at Cambridge. It follows two wartime friends — Archie Jones (English) and Samad Iqbal (Bangladeshi) — and their families in multicultural London from 1975 to 2000. The novel was auctioned for a six-figure advance based on 80 pages. Critics called it a masterpiece; Smith later called its ending 'literary suicide.'","Zadie Smith","White Teeth, Part One: Archie 1974, 1945",38,"Read Part One. Archie tries to gas himself in his car but is saved by a halal butcher. He marries Clara, a Jamaican Jehovah's Witness half his age. Smith writes London's diversity as chaotic joy — her prose is elastic, moving from street slang to Latinate sentences in the same paragraph. Notice the obsession with roots, teeth, and inheritance — can you escape history or are you doomed to repeat it? The novel answers 'both.'",["British","Fiction","Contemporary","21st Century"],"white-teeth"),
]
